Found under k273 (2026-10-31, code reading). `OCP::Cmd::Apply::AMDGPU::ensure` returns `unchanged` before its readiness wait when the live stack matches the spec. So `ocp node add --gpu` of a second AMD node into a cluster whose AMD stack is up to date never waits for `amd.com/gpu` in that node's allocatable — the gap k273 closed for NVIDIA (`setup_gpu_operator` now waits on `unchanged` too, because the new node's plugin pod is exactly what has to come up).

Done = reproduce in a test (stack unchanged, new AMD node at allocatable 0 → today no wait), then `unchanged` also waits on the nodes the device plugin selects, with the same budget and wording as a fresh deploy; t/251 + node-add tests cover both.

Live-confirmed on amdlab 2026-10-31 (image live-20bec47): second 'ocp node add synapse --node_ip … --gpu' after the stack was deployed printed '[ok] AMD GPU device plugin up to date' and returned while synapse had NO amd.com/gpu at all; allocatable 1 appeared ~300 s later. Budget warning for the fix: on a freshly joined node the plugin pod came up ~7 min after registration — Cilium agent Ready 04:15:15, but sandbox creation failed (endpoint regeneration 'buildPermitAcquisition', 'Cilium API client timeout exceeded', 429) until 04:20:44, the same for coredns, nfd-worker and the labeller, i.e. Cilium's first BPF compile on a fresh node. The first join today failed the 300 s budget for exactly that reason ('0 of 1 pod(s) ready'). So the fix must also decide the budget for a freshly joined node (e.g. count from the plugin pod's sandbox, or a longer node-add budget) — 300 s from the AMD step start is too short there. Lib-side idea filed on rex-rancher's board (hostNetwork for plugin/labeller).

2026-10-31 05:00 Reproduced in t/251 (new k286 subtests; a fake node joins a deployed stack, its pods/GPU come up after N s of waiting): on today's code all 7 fail -- the second run says '[ok] AMD GPU device plugin up to date' with no wait while nova has no amd.com/gpu, for the plugin, the DRA driver (no ResourceSlice) and the labeller alike, in the plain step, after the worker join and through node add's _ensure_gpu_stack. The 22 existing subtests pass. Fixing in OCP::Cmd::Apply::AMDGPU::ensure next.

2026-10-31 05:12 Fix (OCP::Cmd::Apply::AMDGPU): ensure's 'unchanged' branch now calls wait_ready like a deploy, on the component's nodes ($for{$c}, every marker node it selects) -- all four components, so the plugin waits for amd.com/gpu in status.allocatable (never capacity), the DRA driver for a ResourceSlice of gpu.amd.com per node (same not_ready as a deploy), labeller/metrics for their DaemonSet. The 'Waiting for <label> on <nodes>...' line moved into wait_ready, so both paths say it in the same words; a timeout dies with the same '<label> not ready after Ns: <why>' and stays a failed component (APPLY INCOMPLETE in apply; node add prints its 'AMD GPU stack not ensured. Run ocp apply' line, as today). A timed-out unchanged component keeps its deployed.yaml record: nothing to redeploy, the next apply waits again. BUDGET (my call): new sub join_ready_timeout { 600 } for a step that runs for a node that just joined -- step/setup take joined => 1, passed by OCP::Cmd::Node::Add::_ensure_amd_gpu_stack and by AMDGPU::after_join; the pre-join apply step keeps ready_timeout 300. Why: the marker is on the node from registration, so the AMD wait starts while Cilium is still on its first BPF compile for that node (synapse: ~5.5 min of failed sandboxes, plugin up ~7 min after registration; from the step's start, which follows node Ready, that is roughly 5.5-6 min); 600 leaves ~4 min headroom and is the figure Workloads gpu_label_timeout already uses after the worker join for the same cause (lab note 25). NVIDIA needs no such change: its allocatable wait starts only after NFD labelled the node, i.e. after the CNI works there. Rejected: counting from the plugin pod's sandbox -- needs pod reads by DaemonSet selector and node, and the sandbox delay is exactly what needs a bound anyway.

